# Auth and authorization

- Keep customer and admin sessions distinct by server-controlled endpoints, token audience/purpose, cookie names, lifetime, and logout behaviour.
- Never use a client-controlled header alone to decide token audience or privileged cookie issuance.
- Customer-facing sessions must not grant ambient admin-console access, even for an admin identity.
- Require role checks on every admin controller and ownership/capability checks in services.
- Protect all cookie-authenticated state changes with a defined CSRF mechanism and strict origin allowlist.
- Cookies must be HttpOnly and Secure in production; document SameSite and Domain decisions per deployment topology.
- Normalize identifiers consistently during registration, login, lookup, reset, and OAuth linking.
- Rotate refresh tokens; revoke sessions after password or privilege changes; detect replay where practical.
- Rate-limit and audit authentication and sensitive admin actions without logging secrets.
- Test customer/admin cross-surface isolation, CSRF rejection, suspended/deleted users, token replay, role changes, and logout independence.
